    "debrief":  {
                    "realTeam":  "About every 26 months, Mars and Earth end up on opposite sides of the Sun. For about two weeks, the Sun\u0027s hot gas can scramble radio signals, so mission teams stop sending commands. Before each conjunction, rover teams upload a simple, safe plan so the rovers can keep working on their own until contact comes back.",
                    "tryNext":  "Turn the Radio off when the silence starts, use procedure cards, and remember to turn the Radio back on before the last sol.",
                    "realScience":  {
                                        "title":  "Why the Sun blocks the radio",
                                        "text":  "The Sun gives off hot, electrically charged gas called plasma. When the Sun sits between Earth and Mars, radio waves have to pass close to it, and the plasma scrambles them. Teams plan ahead so nothing risky happens while nobody can talk.",
                                        "glossary":  "conjunction"
                                    },
                    "sources":  [
                                    "mer_historynasa_images"
                                ]
                },
